    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's get back to the physics.\n\nThe wingtip vortices are a result of the wing's high-lift devices, such as flaps or slats, which create a longer path for the air to follow over the wing. This longer path causes the air to accelerate, creating a swirling motion at the wingtips. As the air swirls around the wingtips, it creates a downward component of the lift vector, which is perpendicular to the wing's surface.\n\nThink of it like this: when you're trying to get a stubborn, slippery fish out of a bucket, you need to use a gentle, sweeping motion to coax it out. Similarly, the wingtip vortices use a gentle, sweeping motion to coax the air downwards, creating a downward component of the lift vector.\n\nNow, when the lift vector is not aligned with the aircraft's forward motion, it creates a force that opposes the motion. This is induced drag. The more the lift vector is tilted, the greater the induced drag.\n\nTo illustrate this, imagine a wing with a high angle of attack. The lift vector is tilted downwards, creating a significant induced drag. The wingtip vortices are",
      "source": "target",
